From: ashipaek0 <folabiklan@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_bcm: max_speed module option

The Raspberry Pi 3 Model B's mainline device tree runs the onboard
BCM43438 on the PL011 at 2000000 baud with no hardware flow control
(bcm2837-rpi-3-b.dts muxes only GPIO 32/33 for uart0). At that speed the
receive FIFO overruns whenever the CPU is late to the interrupt; the
lost bytes show up as "Frame reassembly failed (-84)", HCI command
timeouts and, in BlueZ, an operation that never completes
(org.bluez.Error.InProgress for every later connect or scan).

Add hci_uart.max_speed so the speed can be capped without changing the
device tree, which on EpilykosOS lives on the BOOT partition that OS
updates do not rewrite. 0 (the default) keeps the current behaviour.

---
 drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
@@ -167,6 +167,10 @@
 module_param(irq_polarity, int, 0444);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(irq_polarity, "IRQ polarity 0: active-high 1: active-low");
 
+static unsigned int max_speed;
+module_param(max_speed, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(max_speed, "Highest UART speed for serial-attached controllers, in baud (0: no limit)");
+
 static inline void host_set_baudrate(struct hci_uart *hu, unsigned int speed)
 {
 	if (hu->serdev)
@@ -1568,6 +1572,11 @@
 			bcmdev->oper_speed = data->max_speed;
 	}
 
+	if (max_speed && bcmdev->oper_speed > max_speed) {
+		dev_info(&serdev->dev, "UART speed limited to %u baud\n", max_speed);
+		bcmdev->oper_speed = max_speed;
+	}
+
 	return hci_uart_register_device(&bcmdev->serdev_hu, &bcm_proto);
 }
 
